    "subTopics": [
      {
        "id": "sub-1",
        "title": "1. Anatomy of Institutional Absorption at Key Extremes",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">When a market is trending downward, retail traders place sell stops directly beneath visible swing lows. Institutions need millions in liquidity to build massive long positions without driving prices up against themselves.</p>\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">The hammer represents the exact bar where smart money sweeps those sell stops, absorbs every willing seller at discount prices, and immediately forces price back up into the candle's upper quadrant before the closing bell.</p>\n        "
      },
      {
        "id": "sub-2",
        "title": "2. The 2:1 Wick-to-Body Standard & Volume Climax",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">A valid institutional hammer requires three strict criteria:</p>\n          <ul class=\"list-disc pl-5 mb-4 space-y-1.5 text-xs text-gray-300\">\n            <li><strong>Lower Wick &ge; 2x Real Body:</strong> The lower shadow must be at least twice the height of the body, proving overwhelming buying rejection.</li>\n            <li><strong>Minimal Upper Wick:</strong> Less than 10% of total bar range, demonstrating that buyers closed near the highest price of the session.</li>\n            <li><strong>Volume Surge:</strong> The volume bar must be at least 1.5x greater than the 20-period volume moving average.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "
      },
      {
        "id": "sub-3",
        "title": "3. Shooting Star vs Inverted Hammer: Context Rules",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">The inverted hammer and the shooting star have identical physical geometry (small body at bottom, long upper shadow), but their contextual location completely flips their institutional meaning:</p>\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">At the bottom of a downtrend, an inverted hammer indicates aggressive buyers probed higher levels and tested overhead supply. At the peak of an uptrend, that same shape is a <strong>Shooting Star</strong>, signaling that institutional sellers dumped heavy inventory and rejected higher prices.</p>\n        "
      }
    ],
    "tradeDetails": {
      "setupType": "Swing Low Liquidity Absorption Hammer",
      "instrument": "BANK NIFTY Spot / ATM Call Option",
      "entryTrigger": "1 tick above the hammer candle high on 5-min chart",
      "stopLoss": "1 tick below the extreme low of the hammer tail (-85 pts)",
      "target1": "First horizontal liquidity pool / EMA 20 (+170 pts / 1:2 RR)",
      "target2": "Previous breakdown origin / Day High (+260 pts / 1:3+ RR)",
      "riskReward": "1:3.1",
      "executionChecklist": [
        "Hammer appears after minimum 3 consecutive red candles",
        "Tail tests or breaks an obvious swing low (liquidity sweep)",
        "Lower wick is at least double the body length",
        "Next bar breaks above hammer high within the next 2 candles"
      ],
      "pitfalls": [
        "Buying a hammer that forms in the middle of a sideways consolidation box",
        "Entering before the hammer bar has officially closed",
        "Failing to place stop loss below the exact wick low"
      ]
    },
