    "subTopics": [
      {
        "id": "sub-1",
        "title": "1. Institutional Inventory Offloading & Left Shoulder to Head",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">During an extensive bull run, institutions hold massive positions. They cannot sell everything at once without crashing the market. Therefore, they orchestrate distribution in three distinct waves:</p>\n          <ul class=\"list-disc pl-5 mb-4 space-y-1.5 text-xs text-gray-300\">\n            <li><strong>Left Shoulder:</strong> High-volume momentum peak where smart money initiates their first wave of profit taking.</li>\n            <li><strong>Head:</strong> Price pushes to a higher high, but with <strong>diminishing volume</strong> (a massive divergence). This traps breakout retail buyers at the peak.</li>\n            <li><strong>Right Shoulder:</strong> A weak rally that fails to reach the Head. Smart money unloads remaining inventory and enters short positions.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "
      },
      {
        "id": "sub-2",
        "title": "2. The Quasimodo / Over-and-Under Structural Failure",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">In advanced trading desks, the most lethal form of Head & Shoulders is the <strong>Quasimodo (QM) Pattern</strong>. When the drop from the Head breaks below the Left Shoulder's trough, it creates a lower low (Break of Structure). The Right Shoulder then retraces directly to the Left Shoulder's peak for an optimal short entry.</p>\n        "
      },
      {
        "id": "sub-3",
        "title": "3. Slanted Neckline Geometry & Asymmetrical Volume Climax",
        "content": "\n          <p class=\"mb-3\">Never assume necklines are always flat. Slanted necklines (angled downwards) indicate extreme bearish aggression because buyers could not even hold price to the prior trough level.</p>\n        "
      }
    ],
